---
title: Buttered pasta
description: Lesson 0.2. Boiling pasta properly, and three things people believe about the water.
servings: 2
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, basics]
lesson: "0.2"
technique: boiling pasta
status: draft
---

> Technique: boiling pasta. Salted water at a rolling boil, a stir at
> the start, and your teeth as the timer. The sauce here is only
> butter and cheese, so you can taste whether the pasta itself is
> right.

Bring @water{2%litres} to a rolling boil in a #large pot{}. When it is boiling, add @salt{10%g}.

> Why salt, and why now: salt in the water is what seasons the pasta
> itself. Only a little of it ends up in the pasta. Add it once the
> water boils; crystals dropped into cold water can sit on the bottom
> and mark the pot.
-- source: Caren White, "How Salty Should Your Pasta Water Be?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-08, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5840-how-salty-should-your-pasta-water-be (1 tablespoon of table salt to 4 quarts of water per pound of pasta)

> Science: salt does not make the water boil usefully hotter or
> sooner. In a test, salted and unsalted pots came to a boil in the
> same time, and it takes about 1.5 tablespoons of salt per quart, far
> more than you would cook with, to raise the boiling point by a
> single degree. Look up: boiling point elevation,
> colligative properties.
-- source: Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked

Add @penne{200%g} and stir. Stir again a few times in the first two minutes. Boil for 2 minutes less than the time on the packet, then start tasting a piece every minute. It is done when it is tender but still firm in the middle when you bite it.

> Why stir, and why taste: stirring keeps the pieces from crowding and
> sticking to each other. The packet time is a guess about your pot;
> biting a piece is the only real test.
-- source: "Tips for Cooking Perfect Pasta", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-06-15, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3372-tips-for-cooking-perfect-pasta

Scoop out a cupful of the cooking water, then drain the pasta. Do not rinse it.

Return the pasta to the pot, off the heat. Add @butter{30%g}(in pieces), @parmesan{40%g}(finely grated) and 4 tablespoons of the cooking water. Stir hard for ~{1%minute}, until the butter and cheese have melted into a sauce that coats the pasta. Add more of the water if it is stiff.

> Why keep the water: it is starchy, so it loosens a sauce without
> thinning its flavour.
-- source: "Tips for Cooking Perfect Pasta", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-06-15, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3372-tips-for-cooking-perfect-pasta

> Science: you do not need a huge pot. In a side-by-side test, a pound
> of pasta started in 1 quart of cold water cooked as well as one
> started in 4 quarts of boiling water; it saved 75% of the water and
> up to 45% of the time, and only the most sensitive tasters could
> tell them apart. Less water also means starchier water. Look up:
> starch gelatinization.
-- source: Lan Lam, "Want to Cook Pasta Faster? Don't Wait for the Water to Boil", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-04-28, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7181-start-pasta-in-cold-water (dried penne, linguine and elbows; fresh and filled pasta were not tested)

> Next time, without the recipe: boiling water, about a teaspoon of
> salt per litre, stir at the start, taste from two minutes early, keep
> a cup of the water, and never rinse.
